# The Pacto demo

A complete Pacto fleet on your machine: an OCI registry with real published
contract revisions, an Evidence Server ingesting a signed envelope from a
"remote" environment, and the dashboard showing the operational graph they add
up to.

Everything you need is in this directory. There is no repository to clone and
nothing to build.

## Run it

```sh
docker compose up -d --wait
```

Then open <http://localhost:{{.DashboardPort}}/#/fleet>.

The first `up` pulls two images, publishes the demo's contract bundles into the
registry it starts, and ingests one signed evidence envelope. `--wait` returns
when every service reports itself healthy — nothing here is timed.

The containers run as a non-root user and read this directory read-only, so it
has to be readable by them. A default `umask` gives you that. If yours is
restrictive, `seed` exits immediately with a permission error in
`docker compose logs seed`; `chmod o+rx .` here and start it again.

## What you are looking at

| | |
|---|---|
| `checkout` | two published revisions, one of them deployed |
| `orders` | declares a dependency on `checkout`, and is observed calling it |
| `payments` | never published to this fleet — it arrives as signed evidence from another environment |

Follow the graph from `orders` to `checkout`, open a revision to see its
contract, and compare `checkout` 1.0.0 with 1.1.0 to see a change analysed.

## Stop, and start again

```sh
docker compose down          # stop; the registry and evidence keep their data
docker compose up -d --wait  # back where you were
```

State lives in two volumes, named after this directory. The demo re-publishes on
every `up`, which is a no-op against a registry that already has the content, and
re-sends the evidence envelope, which the Evidence Server correctly refuses as a
replay.

## Clean up

```sh
docker compose down -v
```

That removes the volumes too — the published bundles, the ingested evidence and
the keypair the demo generated. Nothing is left on your machine but the two
pulled images.

## Offline

After the first `up` has pulled the images, the demo needs no network:

```sh
docker compose up -d --wait --pull never
```

Every registry it talks to is the one it starts itself.

